"MuCo: Multi-turn Contrastive Learning for Multimodal Embedding Model (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.06393v1)\nciteturn27147view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.06393v1\",\"lineno\":170}); Total lines: 1892\nL82@P1: to a substantially more powerful and discriminative shared\nL83@P1: embedding space. Subsequently, in the fine-tuning stage,\nL84@P1: we adopt the multi-turn paradigm to standard single-turn\nL85@P1: datasets. This is achieved by simulating multi-turn reason\u0002ing via a prompt-based in-context reconstruction task (akin\nL86@P1: to masked modeling [14]), which further enhances the em\u0002bedding’s discriminative power.\nL87@P1: Extensive experiments demonstrate that MuCo substan\u0002tially outperforms existing MLLM-based embedding mod\u0002els across multiple benchmarks [51, 62], achieving state-of\u0002the-art performance with improved efficiency and general\u0002ization. MuCo achieved new SOTA on the MMEB bench\u0002mark [30], improving zero-shot performance (Precision@1)\nL88@P1: by +3.0%p and fine-tuning performance by +1.6%p over the\nL89@P1: previous SOTA [8, 58]. On the M-BEIR benchmark [62],\nL90@P1: MuCo also secures SOTA in both 2B and 7B, improving\nL91@P1: overall Recall by +1.6%p and +1.7%p, respectively. No\u0002tably, MuCo demonstrates remarkable efficiency: while in\u0002creasing the effective batch size 7 times (1024 → 7168),\nL92@P1: the required FLOPs increase by a mere ≤ 3% (from 17.5\nL93@P1: to 18.0 PFLOPs). This is substantially more efficient\nL94@P1: than standard contrastive learning, which requires 122.7\nL95@P1: PFLOPs for the same batch size expansion, and achieves\nL96@P1: +0.7%p higher performance.\nL97@P1: 2. Related Work\nL98@P1: MLLM-based universal multimodal embedding model.\nL99@P1: Recent studies on universal multimodal embedding can\nL100@P1: be grouped into two main paradigms: CLIP-based meth\u0002ods [36, 37, 53, 66] introduced architectural or objec\u0002tive variations to improve visual-text alignment. In con\u0002trast, MLLM-based approaches leveraged the strong mul\u0002timodal understanding and instruction-following capabili\u0002ties of large multimodal language models (MLLMs) to pro\u0002duce more semantically aligned and instruction-aware em\u0002beddings. Within this paradigm, one line of work focused\nL101@P1: on scaling and curating data for embedding learning, such\nL102@P1: as VLM2Vec [30], MMRet [69], mmE5 [8]. A complemen\u0002tary line of research improved the training procedure itself.\nL103@P1: E5-V [29] unified representations through MLLM distilla\u0002tion, while UniME [21] and LLaVE [33] improved supervi\u0002sion through refined negative sampling. B3 [58] enhanced\nL104@P1: scalability via optimized batch construction, and M3Task\u0002UEM [56] introduced task-adaptive representation learning\nL105@P1: to improve embedding effectiveness across diverse tasks.\nL106@P1: While these models advance the field, they predominantly\nL107@P1: rely on single-turn supervision.\nL108@P1: Training data for the universal multimodal embeddings.\nL109@P1: Advances in data construction have strongly shaped uni\u0002versal multimodal embedding. Early web-crawled datasets\nL110@P1: like DataComp [18], Conceptual Captions [5, 55], and\nL111@P1: YFCC [59] enabled large-scale alignment but suffered from\nL112@P1: noise and lacked semantic richness. The next genera\u0002tion [20, 67, 69] introduced synthetic captioning for exist\u0002ing images [67, 69] or synthesized images [20], improv\u0002ing linguistic diversity and grounding quality. Later ef\u0002forts focused on large-scale aggregation. MMEB bench\u0002mark [30] unified 36 datasets across four meta-tasks, and\nL113@P1: M-BEIR [62] consists of 16 retrieval datasets across 8 re\u0002trieval tasks. These two benchmarks are widely used to\nL114@P1: evaluate universal multimodal embeddings. More recently,\nL115@P1: mmE5 [8] moved beyond aggregation by automatically gen\u0002erating aligned supervision, addressing data insufficiency,\nL116@P1-2: and extending to multilingual domains. Despite these ad\u0002vances, most data still relies on single query-target pairs.Recent MLLM-based embedding models focus on one-shot\nL117@P2: descriptions or isolated instructions, leaving their multi-turn\nL118@P2: conversational nature underexploited. To address this gap,\nL119@P2: our work explores how multi-turn, dialogue-inspired su\u0002pervision can enhance contextual alignment and instruction\nL120@P2: grounding in universal multimodal embedding learning.\nL121@P2: 3. Preliminary\nL122@P2: Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) [2, 34,\nL123@P2: 35, 41, 60] are generative models that extend beyond text\u0002only inputs, processing a combination of images and text\nL124@P2: to produce sequential text tokens. Employing MLLMs as\nL125@P2: the backbone for multimodal embedding models is highly\nL126@P2: advantageous, as this enables the creation of unified em\u0002beddings from free-form multimodal samples. However,\nL127@P2: a fundamental challenge arises from the inherent asymme\u0002try between modalities. The multimodal embedding mod\u0002els still rely on a visual encoder that converts each im\u0002age into a dense sequence of tokens. For instance, on the\nL128@P2: MMEB benchmark [30], an image with the average reso\u0002lution (509 × 456, 294 tokens) requires 2.24 TFLOPs to\nL129@P2: process, while an average-length text (25 tokens) requires\nL130@P2: only 0.12 TFLOPs1. This token length imbalance makes\nL131@P2: the visual input far more computationally costly to process,\nL132@P2: creating a significant bottleneck for training efficiency.\nL133@P2: Contrastive learning. Previous methods [7, 8, 21, 30, 38,\nL134@P2: 58, 69] for training multimodal embedding models typically\nL135@P2: rely on a contrastive learning objective, InfoNCE loss [52],\nL136@P2: applied to single query-target pairs, where query and target\nL137@P2: consist of multimodal data such as images and texts:\nL138@P2: L =\nL139@P2: 1\nL140@P2: |B|\nL141@P2: X\nL142@P2: (qi,pi)∈B\nL143@P2: − log ϕ(qi\nL144@P2: , pi)\nL145@P2: P\nL146@P2: p∈N ∪{pi}\nL147@P2: ϕ(qi, p)\nL148@P2: , (1)\nL149@P2: where qiis a query and piis its positive target. The sim\u0002ilarity score is given by ϕ(qi\nL150@P2: , pi) = exp(f(qi)\nL151@P2: ⊤f(pi)/τ ),\nL152@P2: where f(·) denotes an encoder producing ℓ2-normalized\nL153@P2: embeddings and τ is the temperature. B denotes the mini\u0002batch, and N represents the set of valid negative targets\nL154@P2: within the batch. While using Eq. (1) has become the de\nL155@P2: facto standard for contrastive learning, its effectiveness de\u0002pends heavily on the batch size [6, 9, 19, 53, 66], as larger\nL156@P2: batches provide more negative samples, which further leads\nL157@P2: to severe scalability issues in multimodal training.\nL158@P2: 4. Method\nL159@P2: This section introduces our proposed method Multi-turn\nL160@P2: Contrastive learning (MuCo). MuCo capitalizes on the in\u0002herent causal processing capability of MLLMs, extending it\nL161@P2: to sequential multi-turn input processing such that each em\u0002bedding (for each query) reflects the contextual dependen\u00021Based on Qwen2-VL-2B [60]\nL162@P2: cies of all prior turns. In effect, the model is compelled to\nL163@P2: internalize richer and coherent entangled embeddings that\nL164@P2: extend beyond conventional single-query alignment.\nL165@P2: We implement MuCo through a two-stage training pro\u0002cess. In the first pretraining stage (§4.1), the model is\nL166@P2: trained on our newly constructed Multi-Modal Multi-Turn\nL167@P2: (M3T) dataset. This large-scale, dialogue-oriented cor\u0002pus, containing multiple query-target pairs per image, en\u0002ables the model to learn contextually coherent represen\u0002tations from conversational data. Subsequently, the fine\u0002tuning stage extends the proposed MuCo framework on\nL168@P2: standard single-turn-based benchmarks, ensuring its effec\u0002tiveness under conventional evaluation protocols (§4.2).\nL169@P2: 4.1. Multi-turn Contrastive Learning (MuCo)\nL170@P2: Our method reframes the embedding extraction process as\nL171@P2: a multi-turn dialogue. By placing a special embedding to\u0002ken (<|emb|>) in the response of the assistant within\nL172@P2: a prompt structure, as shown in Fig. 2, our framework can\nL173@P2: extract multiple distinct embeddings from a single input se\u0002quence at once. Subsequent turns per image consist of ap\u0002pending text-only queries or positive targets.\nL174@P2: We first instantiate MuCo in pretraining by compound\u0002ing supervisory signals across sequential turns. Our goal is\nL175@P2: to learn context-rich embeddings for earlier turns – particu\u0002larly for the initial turn2 – that generalize effectively across\nL176@P2: tasks such as classification, retrieval, and visual question an\u0002swering. We believe this could be achieved by training on\nL177@P2: multiple query-target pairs, processed sequentially via our\nL178@P2: dialogue template (Fig. 2); this design ensures that earlier\nL179@P2: embeddings become effective through training subsequent\nL180@P2: turns, as they are continually referenced by later queries\nL181@P2: (e.g., the n-th embedding is formed after attending to the\nL182@P2: previous n − 1 queries).\nL183@P2: Let the set of training data be denoted as D =\nL184@P2: {((Ii, q\nL185@P2: j\nL186@P2: i\nL187@P2: ), p\nL188@P2: j\nL189@P2: i\nL190@P2: )} where Iiis an i-th image and q\nL191@P2: j\nL192@P2: i\nL193@P2: , p\nL194@P2: j\nL195@P2: i\nL196@P2: repre\u0002sent j-th query text and positive target text. For a mini-batch\nL197@P2: B ⊂ D, employing the traditional contrastive learning loss\nL198@P2: Eq. (1) gives\nL199@P2: 1\nL200@P2: |B|\nL201@P2: X\nL202@P2: ((Ii,q\nL203@P2: j\nL204@P2: i\nL205@P2: ),p\nL206@P2: j\nL207@P2: i\nL208@P2: )∈B\nL209@P2: − log ϕ((Ii\nL210@P2: , q\nL211@P2: j\nL212@P2: i\nL213@P2: ), p\nL214@P2: j\nL215@P2: i\nL216@P2: )\nL217@P2: P\nL218@P2: p∈N ∪{p\nL219@P2: j\nL220@P2: i\nL221@P2: }\nL222@P2: ϕ((Ii, q\nL223@P2: j\nL224@P2: i\nL225@P2: ), p)\nL226@P2: . (2)\nL227@P2: We believe this naive approach has two drawbacks. First,\nL228@P2: it often fails to leverage the full context of multiple query\u0002target pairs for a given single image, as trained exclusively\nL229@P2: in a single-query setting, despite the fact that these pairs are\nL230@P2: not mutually exclusive (i.e., crucial cues may lie in other\nL231@P2: questions and answers). Second, when increasing the num\u0002ber of images by enlarging the batch size, it usually fails to\nL232@P2: mitigate the computational overhead incurred by the image\nL233@P2: 2We refer to the position of the first embedding token (<|emb|>) as\nL234@P2-3: the initial or earliest turn, which is only used for inference later.Multimodal Large Language Models\nL235@P3: User: What style of fireplace is\nL236@P3: present and what is its primary\nL237@P3: construction material?\nL238@P3: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL239@P3: Query1\nL240@P3: User: Describe the large\nL241@P3: seating arrangement that\nL242@P3: dominates the room.\nL243@P3: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL244@P3: Query2\nL245@P3: User: Describe every\nL246@P3: significant element\nL247@P3: visible in the space.\nL248@P3: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL249@P3: QueryK\nL250@P3: User: It is a traditional\nL251@P3: fireplace with a red brick\nL252@P3: surround.\nL253@P3: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL254@P3: User: It is a large, L-shaped\nL255@P3: sectional sofa upholstered in\nL256@P3: tan leather.\nL257@P3: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL258@P3: User: This warm and inviting\nL259@P3: living room features an open\u0002plan layout connecting…\nL260@P3: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL261@P3: Sample1\nL262@P3: … …\nL263@P3: Multimodal Large Language Models\nL264@P3: Target1 Target2 TargetK\nL265@P3: …\nL266@P3: 𝑞ത1 … …\nL267@P3: 1 𝑞ത2\nL268@P3: 1 𝑞ത𝑁1\nL269@P3: SampleN\nL270@P3: Sample1\nL271@P3: SampleN\nL272@P3: 𝑞ത1 …\nL273@P3: 2 𝑞ത2\nL274@P3: 2 𝑞ത𝑁2 𝑞ത1 …\nL275@P3: 𝐾 𝑞ത2\nL276@P3: 𝐾 𝑞ത𝑁𝐾 𝑝1\nL277@P3: ҧ …\nL278@P3: 1 𝑝ҧ2\nL279@P3: 1 𝑝𝑁ҧ1 𝑝1\nL280@P3: ҧ …\nL281@P3: 2 𝑝ҧ2\nL282@P3: 2 𝑝𝑁ҧ2 𝑝1\nL283@P3: ҧ …\nL284@P3: 𝐾 𝑝ҧ2\nL285@P3: 𝐾 𝑝𝑁ҧ𝐾\nL286@P3: …\nL287@P3: …\nL288@P3: … … …\nL289@P3: Positive pair\nL290@P3: Negative pairs\nL291@P3: Turn1 Turn2 TurnK Turn1 Turn2 TurnK\nL292@P3: Figure 2. Overview of MuCo. With a multiple query-target paired dataset, MuCo intuitively structures the input by sequentially arranging\nL293@P3: the pairs as distinct dialogue turns. Lines are drawn between extracted embeddings, where blue lines denote a positive pair and orange\nL294@P3: lines denote negative pairs. For clarity, lines are shown originating only from the earliest turn of the first sample in the batch. Solid\nL295@P3: lines represent the pair set used by conventional methods, while dotted lines represent the augmented pairs (i.e., more learning signals)\nL296@P3: contributed by MuCo framework. For visual clarity, we omit the embedding function notation (e.g. f(·)).\nL297@P3: parts. To address these drawbacks, we propose concatenat\u0002ing queries and positive targets that share the same image\nL298@P3: using the prompt template shown in Fig. 2:\nL299@P3: q¯\nL300@P3: j\nL301@P3: i = (Ii\nL302@P3: ,(q\nL303@P3: l\nL304@P3: i\nL305@P3: )l≤j ), p¯\nL306@P3: j\nL307@P3: i = (p\nL308@P3: l\nL309@P3: i\nL310@P3: )l≤j . (3)\nL311@P3: This cumulative structure enables processing multiple\nL312@P3: queries and positive targets in a single forward pass. By\nL313@P3: placing <|emb|> tokens at the end of each turn, we simul\u0002taneously extract all embeddings without re-encoding the\nL314@P3: image, significantly reducing computational overhead. Fur\u0002thermore, gradients from all subsequent turns could flow\nL315@P3: back to the initial turn. The loss function for multiple\nL316@P3: queries and targets is changed as\nL317@P3: LMuCo =\nL318@P3: 1\nL319@P3: |B|\nL320@P3: X\nL321@P3: (¯q\nL322@P3: j\nL323@P3: i\nL324@P3: ,p¯\nL325@P3: j\nL326@P3: i\nL327@P3: )∈B\nL328@P3: − log ϕ(¯q\nL329@P3: j\nL330@P3: i\nL331@P3: , p¯\nL332@P3: j\nL333@P3: i\nL334@P3: )\nL335@P3: P\nL336@P3: p¯∈Ni∪{p¯\nL337@P3: j\nL338@P3: i\nL339@P3: }\nL340@P3: ϕ(¯q\nL341@P3: j\nL342@P3: i\nL343@P3: , p¯)\nL344@P3: , (4)\nL345@P3: s.t. Ni = {p¯\nL346@P3: l\nL347@P3: k\nL348@P3: | (·, p¯\nL349@P3: l\nL350@P3: k\nL351@P3: ) ∈ B, k ̸= i}. (5)\nL352@P3: Note that to address the second drawback, the semantic\nL353@P3: overlap issue, we exclude all other positive targets origi\u0002nating from the same image Ii as the current query q¯\nL354@P3: j\nL355@P3: i\nL356@P3: from\nL357@P3: its negative set Ni (the denominator in Eq. (4)). This ex\u0002clusion is practically achieved by adding negative infinity\nL358@P3: to the corresponding values in the logit matrix for the pairs\nL359@P3: to be masked, as shown in Fig. 3 (b). This approach is bet\u0002ter than Eq. (2) in optimizing computation overheads. For\nL360@P3: example, with a batch size of 1,024 images and 7 pairs per\nL361@P3: image, our method provides each query with 7,161 effective\nL362@P3: negative samples (1024 × 7 − 7).\nL363@P3: In this way, MuCo inherently processes inputs as sequen\u0002tial turns, where each subsequent embedding is conditioned\nL364@P3: on the context of all preceding turns, enabling the model\nL365@P3: to learn diversified and context-aware relationships between\nL366@P3: multiple queries and a given image. From another perspec\u0002tive, gradients from later turns retroactively refine earlier\nL367@P3: ones, yielding a compounded supervisory signal that en\u0002courages the initial representations to remain informative\nL368@P3: across the entire dialogue.\nL369@P3: Dialogue-templated data (M3T). We construct synthetic\nL370@P3: corpora, named M3T, to train MuCo effectively, as such\nL371@P3: dialogue-oriented multimodal structures are publicly un\u0002available. Our key design principles are: we prioritize a\nL372@P3: large scale and multiple query-target pairs for an image\nL373@P3: for versatility and diversity, and structure the data to re\u0002flect comprehensive visual information, ensuring a thor\u0002ough interpretation of both global and local image contexts.\nL374@P3: Furthermore, to maximize computational efficiency during\nL375@P3: training, we restrict positive targets to text-only formats,\nL376@P3: while images are exclusively used as part of the queries.\nL377@P3: We take 5 million images sampled from DataComp [18],\nL378@P3: processed through a two-step pipeline combining state-of\u0002the-art MLLM [2] and LLM [1]. First, we employ the\nL379@P3: MLLM to generate dense captions, distilling rich visual in\u0002formation into text. Second, we let the LLM synthesize\nL380@P3: seven diverse query-target pairs per image using the dense\nL381@P3: captions, aligned with MMEB core categories[30]: one for\nL382@P3: classification, one for retrieval, and five distinct VQA pairs\nL383@P3: composed of two requiring holistic understanding, two fo\u0002cusing on localized details, and one demanding creative rea\u0002soning. This process yields 35 million query-target text\nL384@P3: pairs and 5 million images. Further details and prompts are\nL385@P3: provided in the Sec. D.\nL386@P3: 4.2. Single-turn Adaptation of MuCo\nL387@P3: Our multi-turn pretraining generalizes to the conventional\nL388@P3: multimodal benchmarks [30, 62], having a single query\u0002target pair per sample. To align with single-query data, we\nL389@P3: introduce an adaptive strategy that employs extra contex\u0002tual dialogue from a given query, allowing MuCo to elicit\nL390@P3: its pre-trained multi-turn capability.\nL391@P3: We repurpose MuCo to handle single-query pairs. As\nL392@P3: shown in Fig. 4, we first establish an initial turn to extract\nL393@P3: the initial embedding from the query q or positive target\nL394@P3: p. In the following turns, we provide the model with its\nL395@P3: masked counterpart (e.g., the masked positive target for a\nL396@P3: given query). The model is then explicitly instructed to first\nL397@P3: reconstruct the masked parts and generate a subsequent em\u0002bedding capturing the context of the entire dialogue.\nL398@P3-4: We argue this process functions as an in-context recon\u0002struction task, compelling the model to reason about the re-𝑞ത1\nL399@P4: 1 + -∞ -∞ -∞ - - - -\nL400@P4: -∞ + -∞ -∞ - - - -\nL401@P4: -∞ -∞ + -∞ - - - -\nL402@P4: -∞ -∞ -∞ + - - - -\nL403@P4: - - - - + -∞ -∞ -∞\nL404@P4: - - - - -∞ + -∞ -∞\nL405@P4: - - - - -∞ -∞ + -∞\nL406@P4: - - - - -∞ -∞ -∞ +\nL407@P4: 𝑝ҧ1\nL408@P4: 1\nL409@P4: 𝑞ത1\nL410@P4: 2\nL411@P4: 𝑞ത1\nL412@P4: 3\nL413@P4: 𝑞ത2\nL414@P4: 1\nL415@P4: 𝑞ത2\nL416@P4: 2\nL417@P4: 𝑞ത2\nL418@P4: 3\nL419@P4: 𝑝ҧ1\nL420@P4: 2 𝑝ҧ1\nL421@P4: 3 𝑝ҧ2\nL422@P4: 1 𝑝ҧ22 𝑝ҧ2\nL423@P4: 3\nL424@P4: + - - -\nL425@P4: - + - -\nL426@P4: - - + -\nL427@P4: - - - +\nL428@P4: 𝑞1\nL429@P4: 𝑞2\nL430@P4: 𝑝1 𝑝2\nL431@P4: (a) Logit matrix without MuCo\nL432@P4: (Batch size:4, # turns:1)\nL433@P4: (b) Logit matrix with MuCo\nL434@P4: (Batch size:2, # turns: 4)\nL435@P4: 𝑞ത2\nL436@P4: 4\nL437@P4: 𝑞ത1\nL438@P4: 4\nL439@P4: 𝑝ҧ2\nL440@P4: 4 𝑝ҧ1\nL441@P4: 4\nL442@P4: 𝑞3\nL443@P4: 𝑞4\nL444@P4: 𝑝3 𝑝4\nL445@P4: Figure 3. Logit masking strategy in our MuCo framework. (a)\nL446@P4: The conventional method with a batch size of N = 4 yields a\nL447@P4: N × N (i.e. 4 × 4) matrix. In contrast, MuCo (b) uses a batch size\nL448@P4: of N = 2 and k = 4 turns to construct a larger N k × N k (i.e.\nL449@P4: 8 × 8) matrix. Crucially, our method masks out pairs originating\nL450@P4: from the same image (gray, −∞) to prevent a semantic overlap\nL451@P4: issue. True positives (blue, +) and true negatives (orange, −) are\nL452@P4: used for the loss. Crucially, other pairs originating from the same\nL453@P4: image (gray, −∞) are masked to prevent a semantic overlap issue.\nL454@P4: lationship between the query and target to infer the masked\nL455@P4: content. We achieve this by leveraging LLMs’ capabilities\nL456@P4: through prompting (i.e., instructing the LLM to perform re\u0002construction) and a special mask token (<|mask|>); inter\u0002estingly, even simple tokens such as a space or ‘ ’ work\nL457@P4: equally well. This prompt-only approach avoids auxil\u0002iary objectives, ensuring simplicity and direct application\nL458@P4: to standard contrastive learning. To maintain the efficiency\nL459@P4: of text-only subsequent turns, we convert any images in the\nL460@P4: counterpart to text via image captioning. This conversion\nL461@P4: is performed offline, prior to finetuning, using a image cap\u0002tioning model. This approach offers flexibility, as any suit\u0002able captioning model can be employed for this preprocess\u0002ing step (See Tab 8). Crucially, we use only the initial em\u0002bedding without the subsequent turn to align with standard\nL462@P4: single-query test scenarios [30, 62].\nL463@P4: Details work as follows: first, the query q and the cor\u0002responding positive target p are augmented using prompts.\nL464@P4: The augmented query and target (q\nL465@P4: ′\nL466@P4: , p′) is defined as q\nL467@P4: ′ =\nL468@P4: (q, π1, p, π ˜ 2), p\nL469@P4: ′ = (p, π1, q, π ˜ 2), where π1 is a describing\nL470@P4: request such as User: Please rewrite your last response in\nL471@P4: human-readable language, p˜ and q˜ represents the masked\nL472@P4: query and target, and π2 requests a new embedding such as\nL473@P4: User: Reconstruct the previous response, acknowledge my\nL474@P4: query, and seamlessly integrate the answer.\nL475@P4: For original mini-batch B, we utilizes augmented set B¯:\nL476@P4: B¯ =\nL477@P4: [\nL478@P4: (q,p)∈B\nL479@P4: { (q, p), (q, p′), (q\nL480@P4: ′\nL481@P4: , p), (q\nL482@P4: ′\nL483@P4: , p′) }. (6)\nL484@P4: Query\nL485@P4: Replicate and randomly mask words;\nL486@P4: generate caption or resize (if image).\nL487@P4: User: Reconstruct the previous\nL488@P4: response, acknowledge my query and\nL489@P4: seamlessly integrate the answer.\nL490@P4: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL491@P4: Positive\nL492@P4: User: Reconstruct the previous\nL493@P4: response, acknowledge my query and\nL494@P4: seamlessly integrate the answer.\nL495@P4: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL496@P4: User: Find an image caption describing\nL497@P4: the given everyday image.\nL498@P4: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL499@P4: User: A table topped with a vase with\nL500@P4: two pink roses.\nL501@P4: Assistant: <|emb|>\nL502@P4: User: Please rewrite your last response in\nL503@P4: human-readable language.\nL504@P4: Assistant: Image description: A vase\nL505@P4: filled with <|mask|> sits next to an\nL506@P4: antique grinder on a wooden <|mask|>. Find an <|mask|> describing <|mask|>\nL507@P4: everyday image.\nL508@P4: User: Please rewrite your last response in\nL509@P4: human-readable language.\nL510@P4: Assistant: A table <|mask|> with a vase\nL511@P4: with <|mask|> roses.\nL512@P4: Figure 4. Multi-turn template for fine-tuning MuCo on single\u0002pair datasets. We illustrate Query (left) and Positive (right) tem\u0002plates. The initial query (cyan) is reused as a masked target on\nL513@P4: the Positive side, and the positive target (pink) becomes a masked\nL514@P4: target on the Query side. This process simulates multi-turn in\u0002teractions from a single pair, guiding the model to reconstruct its\nL515@P4: counterpart and enrich the learned embeddings.\nL516@P4: The contrastive loss with the augmented set is defined as\nL517@P4: 1\nL518@P4: |B| ¯\nL519@P4: X\nL520@P4: (qi,pi)∈B¯\nL521@P4: − log ϕ(qi\nL522@P4: , pi)\nL523@P4: P\nL524@P4: p∈Npi∪{pi}\nL525@P4: ϕ(qi, p)\nL526@P4: . (7)\nL527@P4: Since the augmented samples are similar to the original\nL528@P4: ones, we exclude them from negative samples in contrastive\nL529@P4: loss. i.e., the negative set Npiis\nL530@P4: Npi = { p | (·, p) ∈ B } \\ { ¯ pi, p−\nL531@P4: i\nL532@P4: }, (8)\nL533@P4: where p\nL534@P4: −\nL535@P4: i\nL536@P4: denotes the opposite form of pi: it is the aug\u0002mented version if pi\nL537@P4: is an original target in B, and the orig\u0002inal target if pi\nL538@P4: is an augmented one. With this loss, the\nL539@P4: supervisory signal flows back to refine the initial embed\u0002ding, enabling it to encapsulate richer relational informa\u0002tion. In essence, our key intuition is to use simple prompt\u0002ing (without any further complicated elements) to leverage\nL540@P4: the MLLM’s capability to simulate multi-turn behavior on\nL541@P4: single-turn data, thereby exploiting its pretraining strength.\nL542@P4: Simple tweaks such as token masking are also believed to\nL543@P4: enhance the discriminative power of the learned embed\u0002dings. We examine several related design choices in the\nL544@P4: experimental section.\n"
